.TH "DATEBLOCK" "1" "October 2026" "Dateblock" "Dateblock Manual"
.SH "NAME"
\fBdateblock\fR \- block (sleep) until a cron\-style moment in time
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBdateblock\fR [\fB\-t\fR] [\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-C\fR \fICOUNT\fR] [\fB\-z\fR \fIZONE\fR] \fB\-c\fR \fICRON\fR [\fB\-i\fR] [[\fB\-\-\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGUMENTS\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.]]
.br
\fBdateblock\fR [\fB\-t\fR] [\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-C\fR \fICOUNT\fR] [\fB\-z\fR \fIZONE\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fISEC\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fIMIN\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIHOUR\fR] [\fB\-d\fR \fIDOM\fR] [\fB\-m\fR \fIMONTH\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fIDOW\fR] [\fB\-x\fR \fIDRIFT\fR] [[\fB\-\-\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGUMENTS\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.]]
.br
\fBdateblock\fR \fB\-h\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBdateblock\fR works like sleep(1), except that instead of waiting \fIfor\fR a set period of time, it waits \fIuntil\fR a specific point in time is reached\. That point in time is described the same way a crontab(5) entry is, so \fBdateblock\fR can be thought of as running a single cron entry in the foreground: it returns the moment the schedule is next matched\.
.P
On top of the regular cron fields, \fBdateblock\fR can also:
.IP "\(bu" 4
match on a specific \fIsecond\fR (not just a minute)\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fIdrift\fR; wait an extra number of seconds after the time is matched\.
.IP "" 0
.P
Use \fB\-\-test\fR to see when \fBdateblock\fR would unblock without waiting, or \fB\-\-count\fR to list the next few times\.
.P
Given a \fICOMMAND\fR, \fBdateblock\fR runs it once the time is reached and exits with the command's exit code; see \fIRUNNING A COMMAND\fR\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR: Show the help screen (with examples) and exit\.
.P
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-test\fR: Do not block; print the current time and the time \fBdateblock\fR would unblock at, then exit\.
.P
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR: Print the current time and the time \fBdateblock\fR will unblock at before blocking, and the time it actually unblocked at afterwards\.
.P
\fB\-C\fR, \fB\-\-count=\fR\fICOUNT\fR: Do not block; print the current time and the next \fICOUNT\fR times (1 to 1000) the schedule matches, then exit\. The first is the time \fBdateblock\fR would unblock at\. A \fICOMMAND\fR is shown but not run\. In the rare case that no later time exists (ie: past the last date the computer can show), the list ends early with a note on standard error, and the exit status is still \fB0\fR\.
.P
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-cron=\fR\fICRON\fR: The full schedule as a single cron string; see \fICRON STRING\fR\. When this is used the individual field options below are ignored\.
.P
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-isc\fR: Read the \fB\-\-cron\fR string as a regular 5 field crontab(5) entry (minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week) with no second or drift fields\. ISC stands for the Internet Systems Consortium cron\.
.P
\fB\-z\fR, \fB\-\-timezone=\fR\fIZONE\fR: Read the schedule in timezone \fIZONE\fR (a name from the timezone database, such as \fBAmerica/Toronto\fR or \fBAsia/Tokyo\fR) instead of this computer's own; see \fITIMEZONES\fR\. The times shown by \fB\-\-test\fR and \fB\-\-verbose\fR are in that timezone too\.
.P
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-second=\fR\fISEC\fR: The second(s) to unblock on (0\-59)\.
.P
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-minute=\fR\fIMIN\fR: The minute(s) to unblock on (0\-59)\.
.P
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-hour=\fR\fIHOUR\fR: The hour(s) to unblock on (0\-23)\.
.P
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-dom=\fR\fIDOM\fR: The day(s) of the month to unblock on (1\-31)\.
.P
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-month=\fR\fIMONTH\fR: The month(s) to unblock on (1\-12, or Jan to Dec)\.
.P
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-dow=\fR\fIDOW\fR: The day(s) of the week to unblock on (0\-6, or Sun to Sat; Sunday is 0 or 7)\.
.P
\fB\-x\fR, \fB\-\-drift=\fR\fIDRIFT\fR: Extra seconds to wait once the time is matched; see \fIDRIFTING\fR\. Use the 7th field (or \fB+\fR\fIDRIFT\fR) instead when using \fB\-\-cron\fR\.
.P
At least one field (or \fB\-\-cron\fR) must be given\. Fields that are not given match any value, except for the ones \fIsmaller\fR than the smallest field given: those are reset to their lowest value\. So \fB\-n 5\fR unblocks at exactly 5 minutes past the hour (second 0), and \fB\-o 3\fR at exactly 3:00:00\.
.P
When both a day of the month and a day of the week are given, both have to match: \fB\-d 13 \-w 5\fR is every Friday the 13th\.
.SH "FIELD SYNTAX"
Every field (and every entry in a cron string) accepts:
.TP
\fBx\fR
a single value\.
.TP
\fB*/x\fR (or \fB/x\fR)
every value divisible by \fIx\fR (modulus)\.
.TP
\fBx\-y\fR (or \fBy\-x\fR)
every value from \fIx\fR to \fIy\fR (range)\.
.TP
\fBx\-y/z\fR
every \fIz\fR'th value from \fIx\fR to \fIy\fR, so \fB1\-10/3\fR is 1, 4, 7 and 10\.
.TP
\fBx,y\fR
both \fIx\fR and \fIy\fR (separator)\.
.TP
\fB*\fR
any value\. It must be on its own; \fB*,5\fR is not valid\.
.P
The month and the day of the week can also be written as names, in any case: \fBJan\fR to \fBDec\fR and \fBSun\fR to \fBSat\fR (ie: \fBMon\-Fri\fR or \fBJan,Jul\fR)\. A range of names can't go backwards: \fBSat\-Sun\fR is refused (write \fBSat,Sun\fR or \fBSat\-7\fR), since it would otherwise mean every day of the week\.
.P
As in crontab(5), a day of the week of \fB7\fR is Sunday too, so \fBFri\-7\fR is Friday to Sunday\.
.P
These can be mixed when separated by commas, so \fB*/a,b,c\-d,/e\fR is valid\. However \fBx\-y\-z\fR, \fB/x/y\fR, \fB/x\-y\fR, an unfinished \fBx\-\fR, an empty entry (\fB5,,6\fR) and a \fB*\fR mixed with other entries are not\. Every value must be within the range of its field; \fB400\-4000\fR never works for any field\.
.SH "CRON STRING"
The \fB\-\-cron\fR string has up to 7 fields separated by white space:
.IP "" 4
.nf
drift time \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
day of week (0 \- 7) (Sunday=0 or 7) \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+  |
month (1 \- 12) \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+  |  |
day of month (1 \- 31) \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+  |  |  |
hour (0 \- 23) \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+  |  |  |  |
min (0 \- 59) \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+  |  |  |  |  |
sec (0 \- 59) \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+  |  |  |  |  |  |
                                      |  |  |  |  |  |  |
                                      *  *  *  *  *  *  *
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Missing fields at the end are filled in with \fB*\fR, so \fB* 5\fR is the same as \fB* 5 * * * * *\fR\.
.P
An entry starting with a plus (\fB+\fR) jumps straight to the drift field, so \fB* /5 +60\fR is the same as \fB* /5 * * * * 60\fR\. Nothing may follow the \fB+\fR entry\.
.P
The crontab(5) shortcuts can stand for the whole string, with or without \fB\-\-isc\fR\. Without \fB\-\-isc\fR a \fB+\fR drift may follow one (ie: \fB@daily +30\fR); nothing else may, and with \fB\-\-isc\fR nothing may follow it at all (a crontab entry has no drift):
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB@hourly\fR: the start of every hour\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB@daily\fR (or \fB@midnight\fR): every day at 00:00\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB@weekly\fR: every Sunday at 00:00\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB@monthly\fR: the 1st of every month at 00:00\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB@yearly\fR (or \fB@annually\fR): every January 1st at 00:00\.
.IP "" 0
.P
\fB@reboot\fR is refused, since it isn't a time\.
.P
With \fB\-\-isc\fR the string is a regular crontab(5) entry instead:
.IP "" 4
.nf
day of week (0 \- 7) (Sunday=0 or 7) \-\-\-\-\-+
month (1 \- 12) \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+   |
day of month (1 \- 31) \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+   |   |
hour (0 \- 23) \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+   |   |   |
min (0 \- 59) \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+   |   |   |   |
                         |   |   |   |   |
                         *   *   *   *   *
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
It works the same as a crontab(5) entry does:
.IP "\(bu" 4
It always matches at second 0\. Every other field means exactly what it says, so \fB* 3 * * *\fR is every minute from 3:00 to 3:59\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
When both day fields are restricted (neither starts with \fB*\fR), \fIeither\fR one may match: \fB0 0 1 * 1\fR is the 1st of every month and every Monday\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB*/x\fR counts from the field's lowest value, so a day of month of \fB*/5\fR is the 1st, 6th, 11th and so on (without \fB\-\-isc\fR it is the 5th, 10th, 15th and so on)\.
.IP "" 0
.SH "RUNNING A COMMAND"
Anything after the options is a command for \fBdateblock\fR to run once the time is reached:
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ dateblock \-n /15 \-\- \./backup\.sh \-\-full
.fi
.IP "" 0
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fBdateblock\fR \fIbecomes\fR the command (with execvp(3), the way nice(1) and timeout(1) do), so the exit code, signals and output are all the command's own\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
The command starts at \fB\-\-\fR, or at the first argument that is not one of \fBdateblock\fR's options (or an option's value)\. Everything from there on is passed to the command exactly as given, without a shell, so \fBdateblock \-n 5 ls \-l\fR runs \fBls \-l\fR\. Use \fB\-\-\fR when the command itself starts with a dash\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
With \fB\-\-test\fR the command is shown (on the \fBCommand\fR line) but not run\. If the schedule is invalid or impossible the command is never run\.
.IP "" 0
.SH "TIMEZONES"
The schedule is read in this computer's timezone (the \fBTZ\fR environment variable, or the system setting), unless \fB\-\-timezone\fR names another one\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
Daylight saving follows that timezone\. A time skipped when the clocks go forward (such as 2:30 in most of North America on the second Sunday of March) does not happen that day, and a time repeated when they go back matches the first time it comes around\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fBdateblock\fR unblocks at that exact moment, no matter which timezone the computer itself is in\.
.IP "" 0
.SH "DRIFTING"
A drift adds a number of seconds onto the time that was matched\. For example, to wake up on the 1st minute of every 10 minute interval (1, 11, 21, \|\.\|\.\|\., 51) use a minute of \fB*/10\fR with a drift of 60 seconds\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
A drift is always measured in seconds and is applied \fIafter\fR the schedule is matched\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
A drift can not be given on its own; it needs a schedule to go with it\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
A drift can not be longer than the interval of the schedule itself\. Only the remainder is kept, so that no matching time is ever skipped\. With a schedule of every 10 seconds (\fB*/10\fR), a drift of 11 really drifts 1 second, and a drift of 10 does nothing at all\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
Inline drifts (in the cron string) accept the separator and range syntax, so \fB0 /5 +15,45\fR unblocks 15 and 45 seconds after every 5th minute\. The modulus (\fB/x\fR) syntax does not apply to drifts\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
A drift list can hold at most 10,000 values (ie: \fB+0\-9999\fR)\.
.IP "" 0
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Block until the next minute divisible by 10 (0, 10, 20, 30, 40 and 50):
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ dateblock \-n /10
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Block until the next second divisible by 5, but only during hours 3 and 4:
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ dateblock \-c "/5 * 3,4 * * *"
$ dateblock \-s /5 \-o 3,4
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Block until 5 minutes past the hour (all three are the same):
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ dateblock \-c "* 5"
$ dateblock \-c "* 5 * * * *"
$ dateblock \-n 5
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Block until an hour divisible by 5 (0, 5, 10, 15 and 20) on the 1st to the 14th, or the 20th, of the month:
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ dateblock \-o /5 \-d 1\-14,20
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Block until 20 seconds after 5 minutes past the hour:
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ dateblock \-c "* 5 * * * * 20"
$ dateblock \-c "* 5 +20"
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Block until 9:00 on weekdays, written as a regular crontab entry:
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ dateblock \-i \-c "0 9 * * 1\-5"
$ dateblock \-i \-c "0 9 * * Mon\-Fri"
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Block until the next midnight, then 30 seconds more:
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ dateblock \-c "@daily +30"
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Block until 9:00 in Tokyo, whatever timezone this computer is in:
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ dateblock \-z Asia/Tokyo \-c "0 0 9"
$ dateblock \-t \-z Asia/Tokyo \-c "0 0 9"
Current Time : 2026\-10\-24 22:05:00 JST (Sat)
Block Until  : 2026\-10\-25 09:00:00 JST (Sun)
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
See when a schedule would next run, without waiting:
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ dateblock \-t \-c "0 30 2 * * 0"
Current Time : 2026\-10\-24 13:05:00 (Sat)
Block Until  : 2026\-10\-25 02:30:00 (Sun)
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
List the next 5 times a crontab entry runs (a quick way to check one):
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ dateblock \-C 5 \-i \-c "0 9 * * Mon\-Fri"
Current Time : 2026\-10\-30 16:53:37 (Fri)
Next Times   : 2026\-11\-02 09:00:00 (Mon)
               2026\-11\-03 09:00:00 (Tue)
               2026\-11\-04 09:00:00 (Wed)
               2026\-11\-05 09:00:00 (Thu)
               2026\-11\-06 09:00:00 (Fri)
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Run a backup at the next quarter hour; the exit code is the backup's:
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ dateblock \-n /15 \-\- \./backup\.sh \-\-full
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Run it every 15 minutes, on the quarter hour:
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ while dateblock \-n /15; do \./backup\.sh; done
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
With a \fICOMMAND\fR, \fBdateblock\fR exits with the command's own exit code once it has run\. Otherwise, or if the command never got to run:
.TP
\fB0\fR
The matching time was reached (or, with \fB\-\-test\fR or \fB\-\-count\fR, was worked out)\.
.TP
\fB1\fR
No options were given, the schedule is not valid, the \fB\-\-timezone\fR is unknown, the \fB\-\-count\fR is not a whole number from 1 to 1000, or no matching time exists (such as the 31st of February)\. A schedule counts as impossible when nothing matches within the next 400 years, the length of time it takes the calendar to repeat itself\.
.TP
\fB126\fR
The \fICOMMAND\fR was found but could not be run (ie: it is not executable)\.
.TP
\fB127\fR
The \fICOMMAND\fR could not be found\.
.TP
\fB255\fR
The command line could not be parsed\.
.P
If \fBdateblock\fR is killed before the time is reached (ie: with Ctrl\-C), it exits with the usual status for that signal\.
